sections.body:
If you put code that might fail into a `try` block, the program doesn't crash; JavaScript jumps straight to the `catch` block:

```js
try {
  const data = JSON.parse("{broken");   // an error happens here
  console.log("This line doesn't run");
} catch (error) {
  console.log("Caught:", error.name);   // SyntaxError
  console.log(error.message);
} finally {
  console.log("This runs in every case");
}
console.log("The program carries on!");
```

- The `error` object has `name` (type) and `message` (description) properties.
- `finally` runs whether there was an error or not: ideal for cleaning up (removing a loading message, enabling a button again).

Use `catch` only for errors you **expect**: reading JSON, user input, the network. Wrapping everything in `try` hides real bugs.

If a function gets bad data, it should stop with a clear message instead of quietly giving a wrong result:

```js
function setFuel(amount) {
  if (typeof amount !== "number") {
    throw new Error("Fuel must be a number");
  }
  if (amount < 0 || amount > 100) {
    throw new Error("Fuel must be between 0 and 100");
  }
  return amount;
}

try {
  setFuel(150);
} catch (error) {
  console.log("Error:", error.message); // Error: Fuel must be between 0 and 100
}
```

`throw` stops the function immediately and sends the error to the code that called it. A `try/catch` there catches it; if nothing catches it, the program stops with a red error message.

